WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
Think of cochlear implant assessment as several complementary views of the same child.

00:00:05.225 --> 00:00:07.305
Audiometry describes hearing.

00:00:07.845 --> 00:00:11.845
Aided testing examines access through appropriately fitted technology.

00:00:12.325 --> 00:00:17.605
Functional observations and parent reports describe how the child uses sound during everyday life.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:24.950
An infant cannot complete the same speech task as an older child, so age and language ability determine the measures.

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:30.430
In a closed-set task, the child chooses among known alternatives.

00:00:31.065 --> 00:00:37.065
An open-set task does not supply that limited answer list, making the same percent correct a different achievement.

00:00:37.995 --> 00:00:42.075
The audiologist assesses hearing and programs the implant.

00:00:42.625 --> 00:00:45.585
The surgeon handles implantation and medical care.

00:00:46.035 --> 00:00:50.675
The SLP evaluates and develops auditory, speech, and language skills.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:54.630
Parents contribute observations and daily learning opportunities.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:59.590
No single team member or questionnaire supplies every part of the decision.

